 Ticking Details adds two columns. "Tumor condition holds in" says whether both models or only one credit the row. Notes name doses with refit support below 80%, and also mark doses above 2.55 Gy (the largest fraction size in the trials the refit used, so an extrapolation), doses whose acute BED exceeds that of HYPNO's 55 Gy in 20 fractions, and the HYPNO test arm.
 
-Details also shows the button "Trials the tumor models were refitted to". It opens a list of the 45 arms or strata of the 19 randomized trials the Bayesian refit used: each trial's reference with PubMed and DOI links, and each arm's total dose, dose per fraction, number of fractions, overall time and whether that time is planned or delivered, all as used in the fit. A fraction number marked ≈ is an average over the arm. Lettered notes give what the columns cannot show: drugs (nimorazole in DAHANCA 6&7, and previous chemotherapy allowed in RTOG 83-13, and no arm included chemotherapy with radiotherapy) and schedule details, for example where a paper reports only a range of doses or only whole-arm times. A star marks the 8 trials that were not among the trials of the 2018 and 2019 fits by Shuryak, Hall and Brenner.
+Details also shows the button "How the models were fitted (Bayesian)", which opens the page of that name (see below), and the button "Trials the tumor models were refitted to". It opens a list of the 45 arms or strata of the 19 randomized trials the Bayesian refit used: each trial's reference with PubMed and DOI links, and each arm's total dose, dose per fraction, number of fractions, overall time and whether that time is planned or delivered, all as used in the fit. A fraction number marked ≈ is an average over the arm. Lettered notes give what the columns cannot show: drugs (nimorazole in DAHANCA 6&7, and previous chemotherapy allowed in RTOG 83-13, and no arm included chemotherapy with radiotherapy) and schedule details, for example where a paper reports only a range of doses or only whole-arm times. A star marks the 8 trials that were not among the trials of the 2018 and 2019 fits by Shuryak, Hall and Brenner.
+
+## How the models were fitted (Bayesian)
+
+This page, opened from the menu, from Details and from every "How was ... fitted?" link, explains in plain terms what a Bayesian fit is and what was fitted. It covers the tumor model (the dose-independent fit and the dose-dependent model calibrated to it) and the early-toxicity research model, and states that late toxicity is not fitted. Each model has a short summary followed by expandable technical details: the priors and fixed settings the fits were run with, the sampler settings, the convergence checks and what they do and do not show. The last sections explain how the samples are used for Refit support and on the Uncertainty page. The priors, settings and checks are read from a methods record that is checked against the fitted samples the calculator uses, and the record can be downloaded from the page.
 
 HYPNO context: 395 patients were randomized to 55 Gy in 20 fractions. Weekly cisplatin was permitted and given to about 75%. Grade 3 or worse acute mucositis was observed in 190 of the 373 evaluable for early effects (50.9%), against 208 of 380 (54.7%) after 66 Gy in 33 fractions at 6 a week. HYPNO did not compare with 35 × 2 Gy. A modeled BED is not an observed toxicity rate.
 
